        "language": "English ✏️",
        "text": "## LC VAE Merge\n\n### 🛑🚫✋ Read this first\n- Only the **decoder** is merged. The encoder stays **vae_a**'s, so the merged VAE still works with every model made for vae_a.\n- Merge VAEs of the same family that sit close. The report shows the **Decoder distance**: under about 10 % is safe, over 15 % the node warns and you get oversaturated colour or noise.\n- Example pair here: **wan21-vae + krea2RealVae** (Krea 2 / Anima, about 4 % apart). Others that work: qwen_image_vae + hdrVAE, flux2-vae + flux2UltrahdRAWVAE.\n- The Krea 2 render is only there to give LC VAE Compare real latents to decode.\n\n**LC VAE Merge**\n- **vae_a** = the base (keeps its encoder). **vae_b** gives its decoder.\n- The sliders run coarse to fine: **start** (tone, colour), **coarse**, **medium**, **fine**, **finest** (texture, sharpness and the output layer).\n- 0 = all vae_a, 1 = all vae_b.\n- 💡 On Krea 2 / Anima VAEs ramp the sliders (0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1) instead of jumping. Flux VAEs don't mind.\n\n**LC VAE Compare**\n- Decodes the same latents with vae_a, vae_b and the merge, side by side, with a 1:1 crop of the middle under each.\n- **sharpness**: higher = crisper.\n- **drift**: difference from the first VAE (0-255 scale: under 2 = hard to see, over 6 = visible).\n\n**LC VAE Save**\n- ⚠️ NOTHING SAVES UNLESS IT IS TURNED ON! Set **enabled** to true.\n- **fp32** is the safest for a VAE. **bf16** / **fp16** are half the size.\n- Saved here, ready for Load VAE:\n\n```\nComfyUI/\n└── models/\n    └── vae/\n```\n\n---"
